# Target C: six-no-root boundary-shadow reduction

Date: 2026-09-20

Status: exact reduction of any hypothetical six-no-root factor-three counterexample to the sharp `D=0` or `D=1` boundary of the five-no-root problem. This also disproves the proposed blanket five-no-root slack-two shortcut. It does not prove the six-no-root inequality, does not reduce the 3,874-state generic-H6 endpoint, and does not close C-II.

Companion verifiers:

- `research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_five_noroot_k5_slack_p9_2026_09_20.py`
- `research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_five_noroot_sharp_boundary_2026_09_20.py`

## Defect notation

Write

`D(A) = |A| - 3 delta(A)`

with

`delta(A) = r_M(A) - r_Q(A)`.

The committed density theorem gives

`D(A) >= 0`

for every family with at most five no-root four-cycles.

A previous reduction proposed strengthening this to `D(A)>=2` for every five-no-root family. If true, that would have closed the six-no-root layer by deletion. The strengthening is false.

## The five-no-root bound is sharp

The new sharp-boundary verifier gives an explicit five-no-root `k=5,p=10` family with

`|A|=27`, `r_M(A)=27`, `r_Q(A)=18`, `delta(A)=9`,

so

`D(A)=27-3*9=0`.

It also gives an explicit five-no-root `k=5,p=11` family with

`|A|=34`, `r_M(A)=34`, `r_Q(A)=23`, `delta(A)=11`,

so

`D(A)=34-3*11=1`.

All of these ranks are recomputed directly over the rationals in the companion verifier. Thus the global five-no-root lower bound `D>=0` is sharp, and no universal five-no-root `D>=2` theorem exists.

For both displayed families, every one of the other 100 no-root four-cycles raises `r_M` and `r_Q` by one when added while the root-0 selection is held fixed. Hence neither explicit boundary family itself has a delta-increasing no-root extension. This is only an obstruction for these two examples, not yet a classification of the full boundary.

## k=5 has no low-defect state through p=9

The second new verifier strengthens the committed five-no-root `k=5` audit. It exhausts every `p<=9` local state whose all-complete rank bound is below two.

Across the exact 20,451 `S_7` five-subset orbits, there are 323,545 such residual states. Their exact defect census is

```text
D=7      127459
D=8       62173
D=9         155
D=10     108520
D=11      19675
D=13       5563
```

The minimum is 7. The maximum squared relation-row norm is 24, and the verifier checks the same `2^127-1` primality/Hadamard criterion used by the established exact rank audits.

Since the crude `k=5` bound is

`D >= p-10`,

all `p>=12` states already have `D>=2`. Therefore a five-no-root `k=5` family with `D<2` can occur only at

`p=10` or `p=11`.

The explicit sharp families above show that both boundary values actually occur.

## Deletion-shadow theorem for six no-root cycles

Let `B` contain exactly six no-root rows and suppose

`D(B)<0`.

Choose any no-root row `e` of `B` and put

`A = B \ {e}`.

Adding one row changes each of `r_M` and `r_Q` by either zero or one. Because `Q` is a quotient of `M`, a row dependent in `M` is also dependent in `Q`. Hence

`m_e = delta(B) - delta(A)`

lies in `{0,1}`.

The defect identity is

`D(A) = D(B) - 1 + 3 m_e`.

But `A` has five no-root rows, so the established theorem gives `D(A)>=0`.

If `m_e=0`, then

`D(A)=D(B)-1<0`,

which is impossible. Therefore

`m_e=1`

for every one of the six no-root rows of `B`. It follows that

`D(A)=D(B)+2>=0`,

so

`D(B)>=-2`.

Thus every hypothetical six-no-root counterexample has exactly one of the two forms

- `D(B)=-2`, and every no-root deletion shadow has `D(A)=0`;
- `D(B)=-1`, and every no-root deletion shadow has `D(A)=1`.

There is no deeper negative defect to search.

Moreover, `m_e=1` forces the rank increments to be

`r_M(B)-r_M(A)=1`

and

`r_Q(B)-r_Q(A)=0`.

So every no-root row of a six-no-root counterexample must be a coloop in the restricted `M` row matroid and simultaneously a non-coloop in the restricted `Q` row matroid.

This is a much stronger necessary condition than merely lying in one of the four crude six-no-root `(k,p)` danger bands.

## Exact five-no-root boundary bands

For five no-root rows the crude bound is

`D >= 5 + p - 3k`.

Also

`D congruent |A| congruent 5+p (mod 3)`.

Combine this arithmetic with the committed slack results:

- `k=2`: the new containment theorem already gives `D>=2` for all `p`;
- `k=3`: the canonical containment theorem gives `D>=2` for `p<=3`;
- `k=4`: the containment and residual audits give `D>=2` for `p<=6`;
- `k=5`: the new exhaustive audit gives `D>=2` for `p<=9`.

Therefore the entire five-no-root `D=0` boundary is restricted to exactly

`(k,p) = (3,4), (4,7), (5,10)`,

and the entire `D=1` boundary is restricted to exactly

`(k,p) = (3,5), (4,8), (5,11)`.

Consequently any six-no-root counterexample must have all six of its five-no-root deletion shadows inside one of these six narrow boundary bands. A `D=-2` counterexample has six `D=0` shadows; a `D=-1` counterexample has six `D=1` shadows.

## k=5 boundary as a weighted spanning problem

The `k=5` boundary has an additional useful linear-algebra reduction.

Fix the five no-root rows and complete every touched root-0 quotient block. Let the resulting relation matrix have rows

`H_b` for the `m` complete blocks and `E_i` for the five external rows. Put

`d = (m+5) - rank(H_b,E_i)`.

Thus `d` is the dimension of the dependency space of the all-complete relation matrix.

If block `b` is changed from complete to a compatible incomplete local state `s`, its row `H_b` disappears and each external row changes by a scalar multiple of `H_b`:

`E_i -> E_i + lambda_{i,b,s} H_b`.

On the all-complete dependency space, with dependency coordinates `(z_b,x_i)`, this replacement imposes the linear functional

`L_{b,s}(z,x) = z_b - sum_i lambda_{i,b,s} x_i`.

For a set `R` of incomplete blocks, let `r_L` be the rank of the chosen functionals `L_{b,s}`, one functional per block in `R`. Then the actual relation nullity is

`d-r_L`,

and direct row counting gives the exact identity

`D = p - 10 + 3(d-r_L)`.

Therefore:

- at `p=10`, `D=0` exactly when the chosen local functionals span the full `d`-dimensional dependency space;
- at `p=11`, `D=1` exactly when they span the full dependency space.

Each block offers at most one compatible singleton choice of cost one and three pair choices of cost two, while complete has cost zero. Thus classification of the `k=5` sharp boundary is a weighted partition-constrained linear spanning problem, not a multi-billion-state local enumeration. This should be the next implementation target for the `k=5` part of the deletion-shadow program.

## Updated next target

The blanket slack-two route is dead. The replacement route is sharper:

1. classify `D=0` and `D=1` five-no-root states only in the six boundary bands above;
2. for each boundary class, test whether any no-root extension has rank increment `(Delta r_M, Delta r_Q)=(1,0)`;
3. if no such extension exists, the entire six-no-root density layer is closed without enumerating all 325,911 six-subset orbits and their local states.

For `k=5`, use the weighted-functional spanning formulation above. For `k=3` and `k=4`, the existing canonical containment machinery already isolates the only remaining values `p=4,5` and `p=7,8` respectively.

## Direct C-II bottleneck

This remains an auxiliary metric-basis route. The direct generic-H6 endpoint is still

`3874 = 3076 C7 + 798 (C3 disjoint-union C4)`.

The state-specific exact forced-segment-order scan remains the computation most directly capable of removing those cells, followed by quantitative sine-law metric compatibility for survivors. No theorem-state authority file is changed by this result.
